    "summary": "The fleetcast app is healthy and I made no changes to the cluster.\n\n**What I found**\n- Both backend and frontend deployments are 2/2 Ready. Both services return 200: the backend's `/api/health` returns `{\"status\":\"ok\"}` and the frontend returns 200. Endpoints are populated and the ingress is in place.\n- The backend had a bad rollout about four minutes before I looked. Its first ReplicaSet (`554685fd7f`) had pods that failed to mount `tidb-ca-configmap` (\"failed to sync configmap cache: timed out\"), and the readiness probe got connection refused. The deployment then rolled back to the previous ReplicaSet (`db6f7b78b`), which is what is running now.\n- The two ReplicaSets' pod templates are identical except for a `kubectl.kubernetes.io/restartedAt` annotation on the good one. So the spec did not change. The failure looks transient: a ConfigMap cache sync timeout on the kubelet, followed by a probe that fired before the app was listening. I did not confirm the exact trigger, and I did not find a spec defect.\n- Backend logs show normal operation (telemetry and dashboard jobs) with no errors or tracebacks.\n\n**Left alone, for your decision**\n- `locust-fetcher` is a pod not managed by Helm (`job=workload`). It polls `http://load-generator:8089`, which does not exist in the namespace, so it logs `Temporary failure in name resolution` every 10 seconds. It doesn't affect app health, but it is noise and may be a leftover load-test harness. I did not delete it because I don't know who owns it.\n- The frontend image is `lilygniedz/orbital-frontend:latest`, a mutable tag on a personal Docker Hub account, not the ghcr.io registry the backend uses. That's worth reviewing for supply-chain reasons, but it is not the cause of anything I observed.\n\nIf the ConfigMap mount timeouts recur, the next things to check are the kubelet logs on `kind-worker2` and `kind-worker3` and whether `tidb-ca-configmap` is being recreated during the rollout.",
